# Document Retention and Destruction Policy

**{{ org_name }}** — 501(c)(3) Nonprofit Research Institute
EIN: {{ ein }} | Incorporated in {{ state }}

## 1. Purpose

This policy establishes retention periods for the records of {{ org_name }}, ensures compliance with IRS requirements for tax-exempt organizations (Form 990, Part VI, Line 14), and provides orderly procedures for the destruction of records that have satisfied their retention period.

All directors, officers, employees, contractors, and agents of {{ org_name }} are expected to comply with this policy.

## 2. Scope

This policy applies to all records created, received, or maintained by {{ org_name }} in any format — digital files, paper documents, email, chat logs, financial systems, and version-controlled repositories.

## 3. Retention Schedule

| Category | Examples | Minimum Retention |
|----------|----------|-------------------|
| **Corporate records** | Articles of incorporation, bylaws, amendments, board minutes, resolutions, tax-exemption determination letter, state filings | **Permanent** |
| **Tax records** | Form 990, Form 990-EZ, supporting schedules, correspondence with IRS | **7 years** from filing date |
| **Financial records** | Bank statements, receipts, invoices, general ledger, accounts payable/receivable, audit reports | **7 years** |
| **Grant records** | Applications, award letters, budgets, progress reports, final deliverables, subrecipient agreements | **Duration of grant + 7 years**, or per funder requirement (whichever is longer) |
| **Research data** | Datasets, lab notebooks, analysis code, data management plans (DMPs) | **Per funder DMP requirements; minimum 10 years** from project completion |
| **Employment / contractor records** | Offer letters, W-4s, 1099s, contracts, performance records, termination documentation | **7 years** after separation |
| **Correspondence** | Official board communications, formal organizational letters, regulatory correspondence | **3 years** |
| **Insurance records** | Policies, claims, certificates of insurance | **Permanent** |

When a funder, grantor, or regulatory body imposes a longer retention period than listed above, the longer period governs.

## 4. Storage Locations

{{ org_name }} maintains records across the following systems:

| System | Purpose | Access |
|--------|---------|--------|
| **Records repository** (git-based, version-controlled) | Official governance records, board minutes, policies, templates, rosters, voting artifacts | Protected `main`; immutable commit history |
| **Boardroom repository** (git-based, version-controlled) | Working drafts, agenda prep, AI scratch work, and pre-meeting materials | Working history only; not the authoritative corporate record |
| **Board communications platform archives** | Official email correspondence, board communications | Retained per email provider policies; exported annually |
| **SharePoint (legacy)** | Historical documents migrated from original SharePoint site | Read-only reference; no new documents created here |
| **Financial systems** (fiscal custodian custody) | Accounting records, bank statements, tax filings | Managed by fiscal custodian |

Digital records stored in the git-based records repository benefit from immutable, cryptographically verifiable version history. This satisfies the IRS expectation that records be maintained in a manner that ensures their integrity.

## 5. Destruction Procedures

Records that have satisfied their retention period may be destroyed, subject to the following requirements:

1. **Verify eligibility.** Confirm the retention period has expired by referencing the schedule in Section 3.
2. **Check for holds.** Confirm no litigation hold (Section 6) or pending audit, investigation, or legal proceeding applies to the records.
3. **Obtain approval.** The Board Chair or designated officer must authorize destruction in writing (email or board resolution).
4. **Destroy securely.** Paper records must be shredded. Digital records must be permanently deleted from all storage systems, including backups, within 90 days of authorization. Git-based records are retained in repository history and are exempt from destruction (commits are immutable).
5. **Log the action.** Record the date, description of records destroyed, applicable retention category, and authorizing officer in a destruction log maintained in the records repository.

**Records must never be destroyed:**
- During any pending or reasonably anticipated litigation, audit, or government investigation
- In response to or in anticipation of a subpoena, discovery request, or regulatory inquiry
- Before their minimum retention period has expired

## 6. Litigation Hold

When {{ org_name }} becomes aware of pending or reasonably anticipated litigation, government investigation, or audit, the Board Chair shall issue a written litigation hold notice to all affected parties. Upon receipt of a litigation hold:

- All destruction of potentially relevant records must cease immediately, regardless of retention schedule.
- The hold remains in effect until the Board Chair issues a written release.
- Failure to comply with a litigation hold may result in sanctions, adverse legal consequences, and personal liability.

## 7. Annual Review

The Board of Directors shall review this policy at least annually, typically at the Q1 board meeting, to ensure:

- Retention periods remain compliant with current federal and state law
- Storage systems and locations are accurately reflected
- Any new record categories are incorporated
- Destruction procedures are being followed

The date of each review shall be recorded in the board minutes.

